Amazon Route 53 Service Notes
Amazon Route 53 is a highly available and scalable cloud Domain Name System (DNS) web service. It translates human-readable domain names (like example.com) into machine-readable IP addresses (like 192.0.2.1).
1. Core Concepts & Terminology
•	DNS: The "phonebook of the internet," a system that links domain names with IP addresses.
•	Domain Name Registrar: Route 53 can also act as a registrar where you can purchase and manage domain names directly.
•	Hosted Zone: A container for records that manage traffic for a specific domain name (e.g., example.com). Both Public and Private Hosted Zones exist.
•	Record Set (Records): Individual entries within a Hosted Zone that define how traffic should be routed. Key types include:
o	A (Address): Maps a domain name to an IPv4 address.
o	AAAA (Quad A): Maps a domain name to an IPv6 address.
o	CNAME (Canonical Name): Maps one domain name to another domain name (e.g., blog.example.com points to the load balancer URL). CNAMEs cannot be used for naked domains (e.g., example.com must use an A record or ALIAS).
o	NS (Name Server): Specifies the authoritative name servers for the domain (created automatically).
o	MX (Mail Exchange): Specifies the mail servers responsible for handling email for the domain.
o	TXT (Text): Used for various verification purposes (e.g., SPF records for email verification).
2. Alias Records (AWS Specific)
Alias records are a powerful AWS-specific extension of standard DNS records.
•	Functionality: Behaves like a CNAME but can be used for the root/naked domain (e.g., example.com).
•	AWS Resources Only: Alias records can only point to specific AWS resources, such as:
o	ELBs (Elastic Load Balancers)
o	CloudFront Distributions
o	S3 Buckets configured for static website hosting
o	Other Route 53 records
•	Key Advantage: Alias records automatically handle IP address changes of the underlying AWS resource and are free of charge for DNS queries.
3. Routing Policies
Route 53 offers several sophisticated routing policies beyond simple A/CNAME lookups:
•	Simple Routing: The default routing policy. Routes traffic to a single resource.
•	Weighted Routing: Allows you to distribute traffic among multiple resources based on assigned weights (e.g., 80% of traffic goes to US region, 20% to EU region). Useful for A/B testing or blue/green deployments.
•	Latency-Based Routing (LBR): Routes user requests to the AWS region that provides the lowest network latency for the end user.
•	Geolocation Routing: Routes traffic based on the geographic location of the end user (e.g., users in Germany see the German-localized website).
•	Failover Routing: Primary/Secondary configuration. Route 53 monitors the health of the primary resource via Health Checks and automatically fails over to the secondary resource if the primary becomes unhealthy.
•	Multi-Value Answer Routing: Similar to simple routing but allows you to return multiple IP addresses; it helps improve availability when used with health checks.
4. Health Checks
Route 53 Health Checks monitor the health and performance of your endpoints (e.g., your application server, EC2 instance, or other AWS resource).
•	They can check an endpoint by IP address or domain name, monitor the status of other Route 53 records, or monitor the status of AWS resources via CloudWatch alarms.
•	Crucial for Failover Routing: Health checks are mandatory for implementing reliable failover configurations.


